      "dom_id": "question-132486",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "A monitor issues a warning signal when an action is needed as part of a production process. Signals occur continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of one every $0.2$ minutes.  Determine the probability of the next signal occurring within the next $0.4$ minutes. Round your answer to $4$ decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "Since the monitor issues a warning signal continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next signal appears using an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the time between successive signals. Since the monitor issues a warning signal every $0.2=\\frac{1}{5}$ minutes, the rate is $\\lambda =5$ signals per minute.\n\nThe times between two successive signals can be modeled as an exponential random variable with rate $\\lambda =5,$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(5).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{-5x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of the next signal occurring within the next $0.4$ minutes is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<0.4) & ={\\int }_{0}^{0.4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{0.4}5{e}^{-5x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={-{e}^{-5x}|}_{0}^{0.4} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2}+{e}^{0} \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-2} \\\\  & ≈0.8647\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
