      "problem": "A customer support team resolves tickets continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate of $1$ ticket every $5$ minutes. If a ticket has been open for $20$ minutes already, what is the probability that it will remain unresolved for at least another $10$ minutes? Round your final answer to **three decimal places.**\n\nThe required probability is\n{{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "Since the customer support team resolves tickets continuously in time, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time until the next ticket is resolved using an exponential distribution.\n\nLet $X$ be the ticket resolving time, in minutes. Since the team resolves $1$ ticket every $5$ minutes on average, the rate is $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{5}$ tickets per minute, and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{1}{5}).\n$$\n\nRecall that exponential random variables have the memoryless property. So, given two positive real numbers $s,t,$ we have that\n\n$$\nP(X>s+t\\,|\\,X>s)=P(X>t)={e}^{-\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{5},$ $s=20,$ and $t=10.$ \n\nSubstituting these values into the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>20+10\\,|\\,X>20) & =P(X>10) \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\lambda t} \\\\  & ={e}^{-(1/5)\\cdot (10)} \\\\  & ={e}^{-2} \\\\  & ≈0.135.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
